//? Title: Brzozowski Minimisation: Actual Subsets and Double Reversal
//? Subtitle: Reachability, exact language comparison and exponential intermediate state growth
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a deterministic finite automaton, construct a smallest complete machine
accepting exactly the same words. Reversing transitions and determinising twice
achieves this without partition refinement, but an intermediate machine can
be exponentially larger. We retain the original six-state example, inspect
its actual subset states, and prove whole-language equality with a native
product search. An unreachable-state counterexample qualifies the first-pass
minimality claim, and a binary-position family verifies exact state growth.
)

\toc

> Original six-state example: two reversals, three final states

The original alphabet is {0,1}, the start state is zero, and accepting states
are {2,3,4}. Zero toggles between the two initial nonaccepting states until
one is read. A first one enters an accepting region; a second one reaches
the rejecting sink five. The language is therefore all binary words with
exactly one occurrence of one. Its complete minimal machine needs three states:
no ones yet, exactly one, and at least two.

Reversal keeps state labels, flips every explicit edge and swaps initial and
accepting roles. It produces an epsilon-free NFA, potentially with several
initial states. Determinisation constructs only reachable subsets. Both
minimisation passes retain an explicit empty-subset trap when it is reachable.
The diagram and subset tables read the actual native pipeline, with no replay.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/brzozowski_pipeline.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const dm::algorithms::DFA input{6,2,{{1,2},{0,3},{4,5},{4,5},{4,5},{5,5}},0,{0,0,1,1,1,0}};
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::brzozowski_minimize_trace(input);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;style.width=3.7;style.height=1.9;
  diagram.node("input",{0,0},std::format("input DFA\n{} states\n{} reachable",input.num_states,trace.reachable_input_states.size()),style)
      .node("reverse1",{5,0},std::format("reverse 1: NFA\n{} states\nstarts {}",trace.first_reversal.num_states,trace.first_reversal.start_states),style)
      .node("first",{10,0},std::format("determinise 1\n{} reachable subsets",trace.first.dfa.num_states),style)
      .node("reverse2",{10,-4},std::format("reverse 2: NFA\n{} states\nstarts {}",trace.second_reversal.num_states,trace.second_reversal.start_states),style);
  style.fill={220,252,231};
  diagram.node("final",{5,-4},std::format("determinise 2\n{} states\ncomplete and minimal",trace.second.dfa.num_states),style)
      .edge("input","reverse1").edge("reverse1","first").edge("first","reverse2").edge("reverse2","final")
      .text({0,-4},"reverse words twice:\noriginal language")
      .save_svg("build/brzozowski_pipeline.svg");
  std::print("| stage | DFA state | represented source-NFA subset | accepts | on 0 | on 1 |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& [name,stage]:std::vector<std::pair<std::string,const dm::algorithms::PowersetTrace*>>{
      {"first",&trace.first},{"second",&trace.second}})
    for (int state=0;state<stage->dfa.num_states;++state)
      std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",name,state,stage->subsets[state],stage->dfa.accept[state]!=0,
          stage->dfa.delta[state][0],stage->dfa.delta[state][1]);
  const auto equality=dm::algorithms::dfa_equivalence(input,trace.second.dfa);
  if (!equality.equivalent) return 1;
  std::print("\nWhole-language equality: {}; reachable product pairs examined: {}.\n",equality.equivalent,equality.pairs_examined);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| stage | DFA state | represented source-NFA subset | accepts | on 0 | on 1 |
| :--- | ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: |
| first | 0 | [2, 3, 4] | false | 0 | 1 |
| first | 1 | [0, 1] | true | 1 | 2 |
| first | 2 | [] | false | 2 | 2 |
| second | 0 | [1] | false | 0 | 1 |
| second | 1 | [0] | true | 1 | 2 |
| second | 2 | [] | false | 2 | 2 |

Whole-language equality: true; reachable product pairs examined: 6.
\image(figures/brzozowski_pipeline.svg)
// result_end
\caption(First-stage subsets contain original state ids; second-stage subsets contain first-DFA ids. The reachable empty subset is a real rejecting state here. Both passes have three output states, as in the original example.)
\alttext(The six-state input DFA is reversed to an NFA with starts two, three and four, then determinised to three states. Reversing again gives an NFA starting at first-stage state one; the final determinisation has three complete minimal states.)

The first subsets are {2,3,4}, {0,1} and the empty set. The second subsets are
{1}, {0} and the empty set. A subset is accepting exactly when it contains an
accepting state of the NFA being determinised. In the first reversal, original
start zero is the accepting NFA state; in the second reversal, first-DFA start
zero is accepting. The two layers of subset labels therefore have different
source state spaces, even though their output DFAs happen to have the same
transition table in this example.

> Why reachability matters to the minimality argument

For a reachable deterministic input A, consider distinct subsets S and T in
the determinised reverse. Choose a state q belonging to exactly one of S,T.
Reachability supplies a word w taking A's start to q. Determinism means that
reading the reversed word from a subset in the reversed automaton reaches
an accepting state exactly when that subset contains q. Thus S and T are
distinguished by a word. Every generated subset is reachable by construction,
so the resulting complete DFA is minimal, including a reachable empty trap.

An arbitrary input DFA can contain unreachable states, so this reasoning need
not apply to the first pass. However, the first pass always produces a reachable
deterministic machine. The second reverse-and-determinise then satisfies the
argument and accepts the original language, since reversing words twice is the
identity. This is the double-reversal principle associated with Brzozowski's
Theorem 13; the report uses an explicit complete-DFA convention.

> Exact equality and distinguishing the three final states

Testing only words of length at most six was a useful original smoke test,
but it did not prove equality of all words. The new native comparison explores
reachable pairs of states in the two DFAs. A pair with different acceptance
flags yields a distinguishing word. Exhausting the finite product without
such a pair proves whole-language equality. Breadth-first exploration also
returns a shortest counterexample, with ascending-symbol lexicographic ties.
Missing transitions in either machine behave as rejecting self-loop sinks.

For this three-state result, exact comparisons between different choices of
start state provide a second check: every pair has a distinguishing word.
The empty vector in the table denotes epsilon, not a failed search. All three
states are reachable, so pairwise distinction establishes minimality as well
as the earlier language-equivalence check.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/brzozowski.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const dm::algorithms::DFA input{6,2,{{1,2},{0,3},{4,5},{4,5},{4,5},{5,5}},0,{0,0,1,1,1,0}};
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::brzozowski_minimize_trace(input);
  const auto& minimal=trace.second.dfa;
  if (dm::algorithms::dfa_reachable_states(minimal).size()!=std::size_t(minimal.num_states)) return 1;
  std::print("| start states compared | shortest distinguishing word | left accepts | right accepts | product pairs examined |\n"
             "| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (int left=0;left<minimal.num_states;++left) for (int right=left+1;right<minimal.num_states;++right) {
    auto a=minimal,b=minimal;a.start=left;b.start=right;
    const auto result=dm::algorithms::dfa_equivalence(a,b);
    if (result.equivalent) return 1;
    const bool accepts_left=dm::algorithms::dfa_accepts(a,result.witness);
    const bool accepts_right=dm::algorithms::dfa_accepts(b,result.witness);
    if (accepts_left==accepts_right) return 1;
    std::print("| {} and {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",left,right,result.witness,accepts_left,accepts_right,result.pairs_examined);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot({double(input.num_states),double(trace.first.dfa.num_states),double(minimal.num_states)},
      {"original","after pass 1","after pass 2"},"explicit states",dm::RGB{37,99,235})
      .title("Original example: six states become three")
      .x_label("double-reversal stage").y_label("number of explicit DFA states")
      .y_limits(0,8).size(900,480).save_svg("build/brzozowski.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| start states compared | shortest distinguishing word | left accepts | right accepts | product pairs examined |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: |
| 0 and 1 | [] | false | true | 1 |
| 0 and 2 | [1] | true | false | 2 |
| 1 and 2 | [] | true | false | 1 |
\image(figures/brzozowski.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original six–three–three state-count figure is retained. The witness table verifies that the final complete machine has no equivalent state pair; it does not infer minimality merely from a smaller count.)
\alttext(Three blue bars show six states in the original DFA, three after the first reversal/determinisation and three after the second.)

> Unreachable accepting states and complete-machine controls

Consider two unary states: start zero rejects and loops to itself, while
unreachable state one accepts but also moves to zero. The language is empty.
The reversed NFA starts at one and accepts at zero. Its reachable subsets are
{1} and the empty set, both rejecting: the first pass has two equivalent
states and is not minimal. The second pass has one rejecting state, as required.
This qualifies the original report's claim that both passes are always minimal.

The shared representations now validate dimensions, counts, start states,
targets and input symbols before indexing containers. A missing DFA target
is -1 and denotes an implicit rejecting sink. Default construction denotes a
valid zero-state, zero-alphabet empty-language DFA with start=-1. Completing
or minimising it produces one explicit rejecting state. This safely defined
default preserves aggregate field order and types; its formatted start value
is now -1. NFA transitions remain epsilon-free.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const dm::algorithms::DFA unreachable{2,1,{{0},{0}},0,{0,1}};
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::brzozowski_minimize_trace(unreachable);
  std::print("Reachable original states: {}. First-pass subsets: {}.\n\n",trace.reachable_input_states,trace.first.subsets);
  const dm::algorithms::DFA reject_all{1,1,{{0}},0,{0}};
  const auto equality=dm::algorithms::dfa_equivalence(trace.first.dfa,reject_all);
  std::print("First pass: {} states, equivalent to one rejecting state: {}. Final pass: {} state.\n\n",
      trace.first.dfa.num_states,equality.equivalent,trace.second.dfa.num_states);
  struct Example {std::string name;dm::algorithms::DFA machine;};
  const std::vector<Example> examples{
    {"default empty machine",{}},{"epsilon only",{1,1,{{-1}},0,{1}}},
    {"all unary words",{1,1,{{0}},0,{1}}},{"no unary words",{1,1,{{0}},0,{0}}},
    {"accepting zero-symbol start",{2,0,{{},{}},1,{0,1}}},
    {"no start, despite accepting states",{2,2,{{0,1},{1,0}},-1,{1,1}}}};
  std::print("| case | input explicit states | minimal complete states | exact same language |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (const auto& example:examples) {
    const auto result=dm::algorithms::brzozowski_minimize(example.machine);
    const auto proof=dm::algorithms::dfa_equivalence(example.machine,result);
    if (!proof.equivalent) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",example.name,example.machine.num_states,result.num_states,proof.equivalent);
  }
  try {
    dm::algorithms::brzozowski_minimize(dm::algorithms::DFA{1,1,{{2}},0,{0}});return 1;
  } catch (const std::invalid_argument&) {
    std::print("\nAn out-of-range target is rejected before constructing stages.\n");
  }
  try {
    dm::algorithms::brzozowski_minimize_trace(dm::algorithms::binary_kth_symbol_dfa(5),{31,0});return 1;
  } catch (const std::runtime_error&) {
    std::print("A first-stage cap of 31 rejects the 32-subset example without returning a partial minimum.\n");
  }
}
```
