First pass: 2 states, equivalent to one rejecting state: true. Final pass: 1 state.

| case | input explicit states | minimal complete states | exact same language |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| default empty machine | 0 | 1 | true |
| epsilon only | 1 | 2 | true |
| all unary words | 1 | 1 | true |
| no unary words | 1 | 1 | true |
| accepting zero-symbol start | 2 | 1 | true |
| no start, despite accepting states | 2 | 1 | true |

An out-of-range target is rejected before constructing stages.
A first-stage cap of 31 rejects the 32-subset example without returning a partial minimum.
// result_end
\caption(The empty language has one state under the complete-DFA convention. The epsilon-only language needs both an accepting start and a rejecting sink. An unreachable accepting state cannot change the language.)

A positive stage cap is checked before creating an excess subset; zero means
unlimited. It is a state-count limit, not a byte limit: source validation,
large subsets and destination-union temporaries can still be expensive.
Likewise, an exact comparison's optional product-pair cap throws when exhausted;
it never reports equality from an incomplete exploration.

> Controlled exponential growth: kth symbol from the beginning or end

The native binary-position generator accepts a word exactly when its kth
symbol from the beginning exists and is one. A prefix counter followed by
accepting/rejecting sinks gives k+2 reachable states. Those counter states
are distinguishable by how many symbols are still needed before the tested
position, so this complete machine is already minimal.

Reversing the language tests the kth symbol from the end. It must remember
k binary symbols. All 2^k length-k words give different histories: if two
histories differ at index j from the left, appending j zeros makes that differing
bit the kth symbol from the end. Thus those histories are distinguishable.
Conversely, storing the last k bits, padded with leading zeros for short words,
suffices. The first stage therefore has exactly 2^k states, while the second
returns to k+2. The table checks both counts rather than assuming every reversal
reduces an automaton.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/brzozowski_reversal_growth.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> position,first_log,final_log;
  std::print("| position k | input states | first-pass states | 2^k | final states | k+2 | first source arcs scanned | second source arcs scanned |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int k=1;k<=10;++k) {
    const auto input=dm::algorithms::binary_kth_symbol_dfa(k);
    const auto expected_first=std::size_t{1}<<k;
    const auto trace=dm::algorithms::brzozowski_minimize_trace(input,{expected_first,std::size_t(k+2)});
    const auto& result=trace.second.dfa;
    if (std::size_t(trace.first.dfa.num_states)!=expected_first || result.num_states!=k+2 ||
        !dm::algorithms::dfa_equivalence(input,result).equivalent) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",k,input.num_states,trace.first.dfa.num_states,
        expected_first,result.num_states,k+2,trace.first.nfa_arcs_examined,trace.second.nfa_arcs_examined);
    position.push_back(k);first_log.push_back(std::log2(double(trace.first.dfa.num_states)));
    final_log.push_back(std::log2(double(result.num_states)));
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().lines(position,first_log,"first determinisation",dm::RGB{217,119,6},2)
      .points(position,first_log,"",dm::RGB{217,119,6},4)
      .lines(position,final_log,"final minimisation",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .points(position,final_log,"",dm::RGB{37,99,235},4)
      .title("Binary position tests: exponential intermediate state growth")
      .x_label("tested binary symbol position k").y_label("log2(number of explicit DFA states)")
      .y_limits(0,12.5).size(900,500).save_svg("build/brzozowski_reversal_growth.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| position k | input states | first-pass states | 2^k | final states | k+2 | first source arcs scanned | second source arcs scanned |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 3 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 3 | 6 | 6 |
| 2 | 4 | 4 | 4 | 4 | 4 | 16 | 16 |
| 3 | 5 | 8 | 8 | 5 | 5 | 40 | 40 |
| 4 | 6 | 16 | 16 | 6 | 6 | 96 | 96 |
| 5 | 7 | 32 | 32 | 7 | 7 | 224 | 224 |
| 6 | 8 | 64 | 64 | 8 | 8 | 512 | 512 |
| 7 | 9 | 128 | 128 | 9 | 9 | 1152 | 1152 |
| 8 | 10 | 256 | 256 | 10 | 10 | 2560 | 2560 |
| 9 | 11 | 512 | 512 | 11 | 11 | 5632 | 5632 |
| 10 | 12 | 1024 | 1024 | 12 | 12 | 12288 | 12288 |
\image(figures/brzozowski_reversal_growth.svg)
// result_end
\caption(At k=10 the twelve-state input becomes a 1,024-state reversed-language DFA, then returns to twelve states. The ordinate is explicitly log2 of measured positive counts, not an ordinary state-count axis.)
\alttext(The first-pass log-state count rises exactly as k, reaching ten. The final count grows much more slowly as log2(k+2), reaching slightly above three and a half.)

For a nonempty complete input with n states, the final minimal DFA has at most
n states. A partial input may need one explicit rejecting sink, making n+1 a
sufficient bound, including the zero-state empty machine. The intermediate
state count can still be exponential. Treating both passes as unrelated
full powersets obscures this final-size guarantee.

The implementation stores explicit subset members in a map and work list,
sorts destination unions, and compares vector keys. Work depends on those
union sizes and comparisons as well as created states and alphabet size.
Ordinary calls discard provenance/reversal snapshots after use; a trace retains
them as owned data and can be substantially larger. There is no constant-work
per subset or constant-byte per state claim. Exact language comparison uses a
sparse reachable product, with map lookup cost and stored predecessor records;
it does not preallocate every possible state pair.

> References and verification

J. A. Brzozowski, *Canonical regular expressions and minimal state graphs for
definite events*, Mathematical Theory of Automata, 1963, 529–561, Theorem 13.
The author's [1989 clarification, reproduced on page 67 of these lecture slides](https://liacs.leidenuniv.nl/~hoogeboomhj/second/secondcourse.pdf)
identifies the 1963 publication and its 1962 discovery. J. Berstel, L. Boasson,
O. Carton and I. Fagnot, [Minimization of Automata](https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.5318),
section 3, discusses the reversal principle and reversal-growth examples.
M. O. Rabin and D. Scott (1959), *Finite Automata and Their Decision Problems*,
[doi:10.1147/rd.32.0114](https://doi.org/10.1147/rd.32.0114), provides the
nondeterministic-automaton and finite-word decision background.

The six-state graph and boundary controls are explicit, and the binary-position
family is generated in the core; no observational dataset or random seed is
needed by the report. Native tests compare 17,626 complete binary DFAs through
three states and 1,000 partial/no-start machines with an independent table-filling
minimality and language oracle. Other tests check actual subset transitions and
counters, exact counterexample order, limits, malformed inputs, zero alphabets
and exponential growth. All eighteen public documentation examples are compiled
and run. Every report block is an editable complete C++ programme, and SVG
figures are saved locally before being copied into figures.
